  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Blocking Reels on Instagram**\n\nI\'m focusing on the user\'s desire to block Instagram Reels. The core concept is creating an app that effectively disables access to that specific feature within the Instagram interface. My current direction involves figuring out how to achieve this technically \342\200\223 whether it involves overlaying the Reels section, redirecting it, or perhaps using some form of API manipulation if that\'s even possible.\n\n\n**Analyzing Technical Feasibility**\n\nI\'m now diving into the technical nuts and bolts of the project, specifically concerning Android\'s capabilities. I\'m focusing on user\'s aim to block Instagram Reels and realized I need to identify *how* focus apps currently block specific UI elements or apps on Android without resorting to malicious code. I\'m examining standard, non-rooted Android systems. It appears that `AccessibilityService` might be the answer, so I\'m investigating its potential.\n\n\n**Assessing Legality and Risk**\n\nI\'m evaluating whether employing `UsageStatsManager`, overlays, or AccessibilityService to block Instagram Reels aligns with Android\'s security guidelines. The goal is to determine if this constitutes malware or an exploitative practice. I\'ve concluded using AccessibilityService to redirect or block a specific UI element is standard and isn\'t considered high-severity malware. I\'m focusing on the legal aspects to ensure the app complies with Android\'s policies for user safety and data privacy.\n\n\n**Refining Implementation Strategy**\n\nI\'m now refining the implementation approach, aiming for a safe and non-intrusive solution. The focus is now firmly on leveraging standard Android APIs. The current direction involves using `AccessibilityService` to monitor screen content, specifically detecting when \"Reels\" or its associated UI elements are displayed within Instagram. When detected, the app can perform actions like triggering a back gesture, overlaying a block screen, or obscuring the Reels area. I\'m prioritizing methods that adhere to Android\'s security model, eliminating memory injection and focusing on screen content detection.\n\n\n**Confirming Android Implementation**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing how existing apps effectively block distractions, and it\'s looking promising. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed that apps like OneSec successfully leverage the `AccessibilityService` API on Android. This allows them to monitor package and UI element changes, like detecting when the Instagram app and \"Reels\" components are in view. My plan is to use this mechanism to identify Reels\' UI elements and trigger a back action or a custom overlay that encourages focus. I can confirm this is permitted by Android.\n\n\n**Verifying Focus App Standards**\n\nI\'m now making sure the app\'s name and description adhere to Google\'s naming guidelines. After reviewing the instructions, I\'ve confirmed I must opt for a literal, descriptive name, such as \"Reels Blocker\" or \"Focus Blocker\", as this is a simple focus app. I\'ll update `metadata.json` and `res/values/strings.xml` accordingly. I\'m also ensuring the metadata accurately reflects the app\'s function for user clarity.\n\n\n**Finalizing Feature Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core functionality. I\'m focusing on the plan: setting up the Accessibility Service in `ReelsBlockerService.kt` to monitor Instagram\'s events. I will register the service in `AndroidManifest.xml` and configure it in `accessibility_service_config.xml`. It will scan for \"Reels\", \"reels_tab\", and related UI elements within the Instagram package (`com.instagram.android`), then trigger a back action or activate a Focus Overlay. I\'ll include configurable sensitivity and blocking modes for an optimal user experience. I\'m confirming all features are fully functional.\n\n\n**Designing Additional Features**\n\nI\'m now expanding the app\'s capabilities beyond simple blocking. I plan to add a mindful pause overlay before Reels for ten seconds. Strict lock mode will block Reels completely during scheduled focus times. I\'m also including a dashboard with a service status indicator and real-time Reels attempt counter, with local storage via Room. Focus hours, timer settings, and streak calculations are also being considered.\n\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 244029445
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
